WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.302
You now have a complete specification

2
00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.471
for how to construct a knowledge graph.

3
00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:07.474
Join me in this final lesson
where you'll define the tools

4
00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.275
that will execute the construction plan

5
00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:11.678
as specified by the workflow of agents.

6
00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:16.983
With a graph construction plan
and a knowledge extraction plan in place,

7
00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:19.386
you are now ready to build a graph.

8
00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.687
In this lesson,

9
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:24.624
you will dive into the details
of the Knowledge Graph Construction Tool.

10
00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:29.262
If you're feeling generous,
you can call this a neuro-symbolic agent,

11
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.932
a hybrid of language models
with rule-based systems.

12
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.934
Let's take a step back for a moment

13
00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.003
to look at what these plans will produce.

14
00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:40.040
The Graph Construction Plan will load

15
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.043
CSV files to produce the Domain Graph.

16
00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:47.247
There's almost a 1 to 1 mapping
from CSV file to node type,

17
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.450
with some extra mapping
to create relationships.

18
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.054
You could probably ask an LLM
with a giant context window

19
00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:58.625
to perform this task, but
this is a very direct mechanical process

20
00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.860
you have converged to code.

21
00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.129
The work will be performed by a single tool

22
00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:06.499
you'll define called the construct domain graph.

23
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.436
That tool will have a bunch of
helper functions that it uses

24
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.438
for all the different parts of
constructing the graph.

25
00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:12.839
So we'll go through each of

26
00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.240
those tools one step at a time.

27
00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:17.243
As always, we'll import some libraries.

28
00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.747
Check that OpenAI is running.

29
00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.551
And also make sure Neo4J is still there.

30
00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:28.254
Perfect.
Now let's start defining the tools themselves.

31
00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:32.058
For domain graph construction,
we're going to create a series of tools,

32
00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.527
each one with very specific responsibility.

33
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:38.131
You'll start with creating a uniqueness constraint
on the database.

34
00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:41.334
Now that we're moving from just thinking
about the data files,

35
00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.336
we have to think about preparing the database

36
00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.338
for the data import that's about to happen.

37
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.874
And the first part about preparing the database

38
00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:50.076
is to make sure that we have uniqueness constraints.

39
00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:53.012
Now in the database, that ends up meaning

40
00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.648
that for any nodes that have a particular label,

41
00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.517
we can say that whenever that label

42
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.652
occurs and there's a property on that label,

43
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.888
that we want that property to be unique.

44
00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:04.424
That correlates exactly with the CSV file.

45
00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.526
that have a unique column ID.

46
00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:09.329
That's going to be a unique constraint
for each one of those.

47
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:10.730
So this utility function is going

48
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.031
to take care of that for us.

49
00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:14.667
You're just going to pass in the

50
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.536
label and what the unique_property_key should be,

51
00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.806
and it'll take care of actually creating
the constraint on Neo4J.

52
00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.809
Now the Cypher query down in here, you can see

53
00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:26.079
CREATE CONSTRAINT, the constraint name that gets passed in,

54
00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:28.781
only create it if it doesn't already exist.

55
00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:31.351
And it gets created for a particular label,

56
00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.753
and on that label we require that

57
00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.156
this unique property key will be unique.

58
00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.759
Now, before we were using a lot of query parameters

59
00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:42.362
when we're setting up constraints like this

60
00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.164
instead of Neo4J, it's not possible to

61
00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.199
pass in a query parameter for that.

62
00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:47.800
So we're going to be doing

63
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.202
a little bit of unsafe work here

64
00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.904
by actually doing string concatenation.

65
00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.207
And we know that that's not really a recommended practice.

66
00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.209
We should have a sanitized function in here

67
00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.912
to make sure there's nothing happening
that's, you know, bad.

68
00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.914
But for now, this will be just fine.

69
00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:03.449
So the next function you want, if

70
00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.351
you have the ability to actually create

71
00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.053
uniqueness constraints for nodes, we then

72
00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.055
want to be able to load nodes

73
00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:12.058
from a CSV file. You can define a function for that.

74
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.728
So here's the load_nodes_from_csv function.

75
00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:19.599
It takes in a source_file, the label for that source file,

76
00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:23.069
which of the columns in that CSV file is unique,

77
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.271
and then also a list of all the

78
00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.407
properties that should be created
from the source file.

79
00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.110
Now looking down here at the Cypher that's created,

80
00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.013
there's a special Cypher syntax
for loading from CSV files

81
00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.715
that are in the import directory.

82
00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.383
So that's why everything that we

83
00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:39.018
were working with were all relative paths.

84
00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:42.021
Here when we're using LOAD CSV in Neo4J,

85
00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:45.191
this file URL that's here is going to end up being relative

86
00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.694
to Neo4J's import directory.

87
00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.696
So we're going to load a

88
00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.397
CSV file with headers from that directory.

89
00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:54.434
and then for every row, in that file,

90
00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:57.070
we're going to call a sub query here.

91
00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.905
And in the sub query, it's actually

92
00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.773
going to go ahead and do the merging

93
00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.676
of those rows.
MERGE of course is going to do a creation

94
00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.079
by first seeing if that node already exists.

95
00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.681
And if it doesn't, it'll go ahead
and create that node

96
00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.384
based on the unique column that we're passing in.

97
00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.087
And then this next line here,
the FOREACH

98
00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.356
is a little bit of unique way of

99
00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:20.026
taking advantage of the property list
that was passed in.

100
00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:22.128
It's really just the names of some properties.

101
00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:23.229
And what we end up doing

102
00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:24.530
is looping through each of those properties,

103
00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.633
and for each of those property names,

104
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:29.535
we're going to set the property value

105
00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.838
on the node that was just created

106
00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:35.041
to the row value that was passed in
from the CSV file.

107
00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.243
So in effect, we're going to loop through all

108
00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.212
the names and go ahead and just iteratively set those.

109
00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:42.749
values here.
Now by using this inside of a subquery,

110
00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:45.051
we then get the option of doing this in batch.

111
00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:47.754
So we're going to do this IN TRANSACTIONS OF 1000 ROWS.

112
00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.256
So no matter how big the CSV file is,

113
00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.557
we're going to do it as a

114
00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:52.859
batch of just 1,000 at a time.

115
00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:54.861
You can then see what we're actually going

116
00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.562
to call to Neo4J, sending in the query.

117
00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:58.865
We're going to pass that query in along with

118
00:04:58.865 --> 00:05:01.167
a bunch of query parameters that will get used.

119
00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.904
Now for each CSV file that you have,
you actually

120
00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.973
want to do both of the functions that we just defined.

121
00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.575
We want to call both of them.

122
00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.177
First call the creating a uniqueness constraint

123
00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.346
to make sure that we'll have unique values

124
00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.481
for each node, so the IDs are respected.

125
00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.583
And then after we've created a uniqueness

126
00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:19.185
constraints for each of the CSV files,

127
00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.220
we're then going to go ahead

128
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.989
and actually do the import using the

129
00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:25.491
load_nodes_from_csv tool that we just saw.

130
00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:28.361
So that's what's happening here
inside of import_nodes.

131
00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.863
It just taking the construction rule,

132
00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.266
and then first based on the values
from the construction rule,

133
00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:35.134
it's going to go ahead and call

134
00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:37.036
the create uniqueness constraints tool right here.

135
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.405
And then if it doesn't have an error,

136
00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.873
it'll go on and do the import

137
00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.375
from the nodes from the CSV file.

138
00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.545
And all it's doing here is just taking
from the construction rule,

139
00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:46.946
each of the different values that

140
00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:48.548
need to be passed into the function.

141
00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:52.885
You can do something similar with importing relationships.

142
00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.587
Now in relationships, we don't need

143
00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:56.489
to apply a uniqueness constraint on those

144
00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:59.492
because they're not going to have
any identities themselves.

145
00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:02.595
They're going to be connected directly
to nodes on either side,

146
00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:06.566
either the from or the to node.
And those should be unique.

147
00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.267
But the relationship themselves are going to be

148
00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:09.769
unique simply because there's just one of each

149
00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:13.072
for every row in the files that we're importing.

150
00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.241
This is a little particular to the

151
00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:16.943
the data files that we have here.

152
00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:19.679
We'd want to take a little bit more care
if we had,

153
00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:22.248
you know, data files that were set up in
some more complicated way,

154
00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.684
but for the data files that we have in this

155
00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:27.019
example, this is an adequate way
to actually do the import.

156
00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:29.856
As with the node loading,

157
00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:32.058
we're going to use LOAD CSV WITH HEADERS

158
00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:35.862
from the import directory and
load all those as rows.

159
00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.663
And then for each of those rows,

160
00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:39.232
we're going to actually do a little

161
00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:41.067
bit more work inside of the sub query.

162
00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.636
First, we're going to find the existing nodes

163
00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:45.338
that were previously loaded.

164
00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:47.240
So we're going to find the from_node

165
00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.841
and we're going to find the to_node.

166
00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.644
And after we've found those using a MATCH clause,

167
00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:55.915
we'll use the MERGE clause here
to actually create the relationship

168
00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.917
from the from_node to the to_node.

169
00:06:58.017 --> 00:07:00.920
So this is only going to allow us
to create one relationship

170
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.523
for any given pair of a from and a to

171
00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.525
of a particular type of relationship.

172
00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:08.127
So if there are two relationships that were,

173
00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.430
say there are two ABK likes coffee rows,

174
00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:12.465
That would only happen once because

175
00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:14.333
even if there were two that occurred

176
00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.469
in the CSV file, by merging it here,

177
00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:18.538
the merge will look at the nodes.

178
00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.640
Those are already unique on either side.

179
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.242
And the relationship already has an ABK likes,

180
00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.911
so the likes is already there.

181
00:07:24.911 --> 00:07:26.846
It's not going to create another relationship.

182
00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.515
And same thing with the nodes.

183
00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.684
We're going to do the property setting
on the relationships

184
00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:33.486
if there were any properties
that are available.

185
00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:36.689
We'll loop through them using
this FOREACH construct here,

186
00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:38.691
and that'll set all the values that are available.

187
00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:40.827
And then just go ahead and call

188
00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:42.728
the query, passing in all the query parameters

189
00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:46.132
directly from the construction rule itself.

190
00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:50.136
So all of those functions do most of the real work,

191
00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:52.338
and then construct_domain_graph itself

192
00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.340
really doesn't have to do a whole lot

193
00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.942
other than to take in as an argument

194
00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:57.743
the full construction_plan

195
00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:01.147
and then do the right ordering of
constructing all the nodes first,

196
00:08:01.514 --> 00:08:03.115
and then now with the nodes already existing,

197
00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:04.917
then you can go ahead and create the relationships.

198
00:08:05.952 --> 00:08:08.354
With construct_domain_graph already defined,

199
00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:10.256
we can go ahead and give it a try.

200
00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.758
Now to run this, of course, we're going to need

201
00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:16.762
a complete construction plan.
So here's the whole construction plan.

202
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:19.365
This will be different from previous lessons

203
00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:21.567
depending on what the LLM decided to do.

204
00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:23.669
When this notebook was created,

205
00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.972
here's the construction plan that was valid.

206
00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:27.907
and you can take a look

207
00:08:27.907 --> 00:08:29.709
through that. It's it's kind of expected.

208
00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:32.211
I think the thing to pay most attention to here

209
00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:35.681
that we'll check later is the relationships that are created.

210
00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:38.885
There is a contains relationship
that goes from a Product,

211
00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:41.487
contains an Assembly. That sounds right.

212
00:08:41.754 --> 00:08:45.291
And the IS_PART_OF goes from a part,

213
00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:47.260
is a part of an assembly.

214
00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.196
Okay, that's a little wordy,
but that's that's correct.

215
00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:53.499
And the from node here is a part that

216
00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:56.369
is supplied by a supplier.
Also seems perfectly fine.

217
00:08:56.869 --> 00:09:01.140
So that will be the input to
our construct_domain_graph function.

218
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.842
So, let's give it a run.

219
00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:06.345
Now, when that function is done,
you don't see any output.

220
00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:07.747
could have added a print statement

221
00:09:07.747 --> 00:09:09.048
or something to let you know that

222
00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:11.050
okay, it's finished doing its work.

223
00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:12.752
Instead, what we're going to do

224
00:09:12.752 --> 00:09:14.353
here is actually interact with the graph

225
00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:16.355
as if we were still doing some querying.

226
00:09:16.656 --> 00:09:19.058
And so let's inspect the domain graph

227
00:09:19.058 --> 00:09:21.193
with a bit of a fancy Cypher

228
00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:23.362
statement. What we're going to do is,

229
00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:27.366
first find all the just relationship construction rules
from the construction plan

230
00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:29.702
and we're going to do that with

231
00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:31.871
just a list comprehension expression inside of Python.

232
00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:35.174
So we're just going to loop through
all the construction plan values

233
00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:39.478
and whenever the construction_type is relationship,
it gets put into this list.

234
00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:41.747
So here's all the relationship constructions that

235
00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:43.983
we saw before, but without the node constructions.

236
00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.152
And then we're going to do

237
00:09:46.152 --> 00:09:48.054
a little bit of Cypher magic here.

238
00:09:48.354 --> 00:09:49.455
And so I'm going to walk

239
00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:50.756
through this one step at a time.

240
00:09:51.057 --> 00:09:53.326
In this fancy Cypher query we're about to run,

241
00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:56.028
the goal is basically to do a little sampling of the graph.

242
00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:57.530
So we're not going to get the

243
00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:58.731
entire graph to take a look at,

244
00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:00.466
but what we want to do

245
00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.034
is we're expecting that for every relationship

246
00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:05.438
construction rule that was up here,
there should at least be one

247
00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:09.041
occurrence of that relationship type
in the resulting graph.

248
00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:10.910
So if we just get a

249
00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.612
collection of all the different relationship types

250
00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:14.714
and just do pattern matching on those,

251
00:10:14.714 --> 00:10:16.616
and then see the output of that,

252
00:10:16.616 --> 00:10:19.318
